+## 197.8 - (Improved) Under-strength military production could still react too weakly for low-BuildUnitProb personalities because the relative-power catch-up was only multiplicative even when more units were affordable
+
+Screenshots/files for this issue: [google drive folder link](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/14XBMZx6zu154YemD5_lrwLZRmYDZXmVW?usp=sharing).
+
+The KI#197 power-per-city catch-up originally multiplied the AI's existing `BuildUnitProb`. This correctly increased military pressure when an empire was weak for its city count, but peaceful personalities with a low base probability could still remain too timid: a large relative-power deficit multiplied into a small base value still produced only a moderate final probability.
+
+To improve this without turning military spending into a quota, the AI now adds a second, absolute response from the same capped power-per-city deficit only while the normal `max+15` military-spending allowance still permits more units. The extra response is controlled by `SAS_AI_BUILD_UNIT_PROB_UNDERSTRENGTH_AFFORDABLE_ABSOLUTE_WEIGHT_PERCENT`; spending is only an affordability brake, not a target to fill. Financial/economy suppression, `AI_STRATEGY_GET_BETTER_UNITS`, impossible-war cases, the existing high-power throttle, and drafting behavior retain their existing gates.
+
+Save file 459 provided a clear low-personality example on turn 200: Mali had 6 cities and only 20 combat units, with power per city about 34% below the relevant-rival average. Its personality/XP BuildUnitProb of 29 rose to 39 through the existing multiplicative catch-up, then to 56 through the new +17 affordable absolute response because unit spending was 0 and the local recommended maximum was 82. Across the complete save-file 459 BBAI run, the new absolute response added points in 2,669 of 5,838 logged BuildUnitProb evaluations (about 46%), averaging about +10 points when active and reaching the intended +25-point maximum at a capped 50% deficit. Of evaluations where the under-strength path reached the affordability check, 439 were denied by the spending brake, confirming that the extra response does not remain active once the army becomes expensive enough.
+
+The change improves military catch-up but intentionally does not attempt to make the recommended unit-spending maximum itself a target or guarantee a particular army size. Save file 459 still showed some mid/late-game armies and especially offensive-unit counts below the desired AdvCiv-SAS pressure, so broader healthy-empire military buildup and unit-composition issues remain separate follow-up work rather than being hidden inside this catch-up rule.
+
+Improved with the help of GPT-5.6 Thinking thanks.

